    <div class="report-container">
        <div class="header">
            <h1>AI Impact on the Global Job Market: Risk, Salary & Skills Trend Analysis (2015–2035)</h1>
            <p class="subtitle">A comprehensive trend analysis examining how AI automation risk, salary dynamics, skill demand, and job survival prospects are evolving across roles, countries, and experience levels from 2015 to 2035.</p>
            <p class="meta">Report Date: April 2026 | Dataset: 12,343 records | Time Horizon: 21 Years</p>
        </div>

        <nav class="nav">
            <a href="#executive-overview">Executive Overview</a>
            <a href="#automation-risk">Automation Risk Landscape</a>
            <a href="#salary-trends">Salary Trends</a>
            <a href="#skills-demand">Skills Demand</a>
            <a href="#job-survival">Job Survival Outlook</a>
            <a href="#recommendations">Strategic Recommendations</a>
        </nav>

        <div class="content">
            <!-- Executive Overview -->
            <section id="executive-overview" class="section">
                <h2>Executive Overview</h2>

                <div class="exec-summary">
                    <h3>Executive Summary</h3>
                    <p>This report analyzes 12,343 data points spanning 2015–2035 to reveal how AI automation is reshaping the global job market. The data covers 10 job titles across 6 countries, tracking AI risk scores, salaries, skill demand, and job survival prospects.</p>
                </div>

                <h3>Key Findings</h3>

                <div class="key-takeaways">
                    <div class="takeaway">
                        <div class="number">+72%</div>
                        <div class="label">AI Risk Score Increase (2015–2035)</div>
                        <p style="margin-top:10px;font-size:0.85em;color:#666;">From 0.295 to 0.508 average risk score</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="takeaway">
                        <div class="number">43%</div>
                        <div class="label">Jobs at High Risk by 2035</div>
                        <p style="margin-top:10px;font-size:0.85em;color:#666;">Business Analyst & Data Analyst face highest exposure</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="takeaway">
                        <div class="number">-$12K</div>
                        <div class="label">High-Risk Salary Gap vs Low-Risk</div>
                        <p style="margin-top:10px;font-size:0.85em;color:#666;">Median: $21.7K (high risk) vs $34.1K (low risk)</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="takeaway">
                        <div class="number">100%</div>
                        <div class="label">Job Survival for AI Researchers</div>
                        <p style="margin-top:10px;font-size:0.85em;color:#666;">Highest survival rate across all roles</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="insight-box" style="margin-top:30px;">
                    <h4>Critical Insights for Executive Leadership</h4>
                    <ul style="margin-left:20px;color:#444;">
                        <li><strong>Acceleration Post-2025:</strong> AI risk scores accelerate significantly after 2025, with average risk exceeding 0.50 by 2035</li>
                        <li><strong>Salary Risk Correlation:</strong> Higher AI risk correlates with lower compensation (−0.24 correlation), suggesting cost-driven automation pressure</li>
                        <li><strong>Skill Demand Stable:</strong> Overall skill demand scores remain relatively stable (78–81), but at-risk roles show degraded demand</li>
                        <li><strong>Geographic Parity:</strong> AI risk is relatively uniform across countries (0.39–0.40), but salary differentials are substantial (USA 3.5× India)</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Automation Risk Landscape -->
            <section id="automation-risk" class="section">
                <h2>AI Automation Risk Landscape</h2>

                <p>The AI risk score (0–1 scale) measures automation vulnerability. A score of 0.50 means roughly a 50% probability of significant job impact from AI automation.</p>

                <div class="metric-grid">
                    <div class="metric-card">
                        <div class="value">0.396</div>
                        <div class="label">Overall Mean AI Risk Score</div>
                        <div class="change">39.6% average automation probability</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="metric-card alt">
                        <div class="value">0.508</div>
                        <div class="label">2035 Projected AI Risk Score</div>
                        <div class="change">↑ 72% from 2015 baseline</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="metric-card alt2">
                        <div class="value">18.6%</div>
                        <div class="label">Records in High Risk Category</div>
                        <div class="change">2,294 of 12,343 records</div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h3>AI Risk by Job Title</h3>
                <div class="chart-container">
                    <h4>Average AI Risk Score by Job Title (2015–2035)</h4>
                    <div class="chart-wrapper">
                        <canvas id="riskByJobChart"></canvas>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <p><strong>Key Finding:</strong> Business Analysts (0.75) and Data Analysts (0.65) face the highest automation risk, while AI Researchers (0.15) and Cybersecurity Analysts (0.25) demonstrate strong resilience. The ~0.60 point spread between highest and lowest risk roles underscores critical workforce planning implications.</p>

                <h3>AI Risk by Country</h3>
                <div class="chart-container">
                    <h4>Average AI Risk Score by Country</h4>
                    <div class="chart-wrapper small">
                        <canvas id="riskByCountryChart"></canvas>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <p><strong>Key Finding:</strong> AI risk exposure is remarkably consistent across countries (range: 0.392–0.401), indicating that automation pressure is role-dependent rather than geography-dependent. India and USA show slightly higher averages, but differences are marginal.</p>

                <h3>AI Risk Trend Over Time</h3>
                <div class="chart-container">
                    <h4>Evolution of AI Risk Score by Job Title (2015–2035)</h4>
                    <div class="chart-wrapper">
                        <canvas id="riskTrendChart"></canvas>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="insight-box">
                    <h4>Risk Category Distribution Shift</h4>
                    <p>The proportion of "High Risk" records grew substantially after 2020, accelerating through 2025–2035. By 2035, Business Analyst and Data Analyst roles are predominantly in the High Risk category, while AI Researcher, ML Engineer, and Cybersecurity Analyst maintain Low Risk status throughout the projection period.</p>
                </div>

                <h3>AI Risk by Experience & Education</h3>
                <table>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Experience Level</th>
                        <th>Avg AI Risk Score</th>
                        <th>Risk Category</th>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Entry</td>
                        <td>0.396</td>
                        <td><span class="badge badge-medium">Medium Risk</span></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Mid</td>
                        <td>0.399</td>
                        <td><span class="badge badge-medium">Medium Risk</span></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Senior</td>
                        <td>0.393</td>
                        <td><span class="badge badge-medium">Medium Risk</span></td>
                    </tr>
                </table>
                <p style="font-size:0.85em;color:#666;margin-top:10px;">Experience level shows minimal correlation with AI risk, suggesting that automation threat is more about job function than seniority.</p>
            </section>

            <!-- Salary Trends -->
            <section id="salary-trends" class="section">
                <h2>Salary Trends & Segmentation</h2>

                <p>Salary analysis reveals significant disparities by geography, role, and increasingly, by AI risk exposure. The data shows a clear compensation stratification emerging between automation-safe and automation-vulnerable roles.</p>

                <div class="metric-grid">
                    <div class="metric-card alt3">
                        <div class="value">$34,554</div>
                        <div class="label">Overall Median Salary (USD)</div>
                        <div class="change">Range: $3,875 – $113,589</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="metric-card alt">
                        <div class="value">$43,422</div>
                        <div class="label">USA Median Salary</div>
                        <div class="change">Highest among all countries</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="metric-card alt2">
                        <div class="value">−0.24</div>
                        <div class="label">Salary × AI Risk Correlation</div>
                        <div class="change">Higher risk = Lower pay</div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h3>Median Salary by Country</h3>
                <div class="chart-container">
                    <h4>Salary Evolution by Country (2015–2035)</h4>
                    <div class="chart-wrapper">
                        <canvas id="salaryCountryChart"></canvas>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h3>Salary × AI Risk Correlation</h3>
                <div class="chart-container">
                    <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:30px;">
                        <div>
                            <h4>Median Salary by AI Risk Category</h4>
                            <div class="chart-wrapper small">
                                <canvas id="salaryByRiskChart"></canvas>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div>
                            <h4>AI Risk Score by Salary Bucket</h4>
                            <div class="chart-wrapper small">
                                <canvas id="riskBySalaryBucketChart"></canvas>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="insight-box">
                    <h4>Salary × AI Risk Correlation Analysis</h4>
                    <p>The correlation coefficient of <strong>−0.24</strong> between salary and AI risk score reveals an important market dynamic: roles with high automation exposure tend to be compensated at lower levels. This may reflect several factors:</p>
                    <ul style="margin-left:20px;margin-top:10px;color:#444;">
                        <li>Routine, rules-based work (high automation risk) commands routine salaries</li>
                        <li>High-skill, judgment-intensive roles (low automation risk) demand premium compensation</li>
                        <li>The salary penalty for automation risk averages $12,000–$13,000 in median terms</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h3>Salary by Job Title</h3>
                <table>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Job Title</th>
                        <th>Median Salary</th>
                        <th>AI Risk Category</th>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
